WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:08.890
Hi, everyone.

00:00:08.890 --> 00:00:10.320
My name is Rachel
Gordon, and I'm

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:13.230
the communications and
media relations coordinator

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:16.230
at MIT's Computer Science
and Artificial Intelligence

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:17.610
Laboratory.

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:21.030
Today I'm joined by AI and data
scientist, Cameron Hamilton,

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:23.730
who recently found love on
the hugely popular Netflix

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:25.770
show "Love is Blind."

00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:28.050
He's the founder of the
startup AI Alliance,

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:30.630
which focuses on tailor
made AI solutions

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:32.400
across all industries.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:34.153
Thanks for joining us today.

00:00:34.153 --> 00:00:35.820
Thank you so much for
having me, Rachel.

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:37.690
Pleasure to be here.

00:00:37.690 --> 00:00:40.750
You're known mostly for
your work in AI and science,

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:43.000
but you recently
ventured into the world

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:44.590
of reality television?

00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:47.550
Do you see any connection
between these two worlds?

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:49.130
You know, that's
a good question.

00:00:49.130 --> 00:00:54.130
I think that "Love is Blind"
is a special kind of show,

00:00:54.130 --> 00:00:56.940
and it's even more than a show.

00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:59.020
It's an experiment in
some sense, you know.

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:02.140
It's an experiment
where the creators

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:05.530
were interested in seeing,
well, if we remove--

00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:08.290
as I kind of said on the show--
these confounding variables

00:01:08.290 --> 00:01:12.570
of physical appearance,
ethnicity, race, background, et

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:15.160
cetera, can people
just make a connection

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:18.040
based purely on the
conversation that they've had?

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:24.090
And I think I was drawn
to the experimental setup,

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:24.810
if you will.

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:29.880
I was intrigued by it.

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:32.090
I thought, you know,
I just want to see

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:35.930
where this goes and
see if there's merit

00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:38.870
to doing dating in this way.

00:01:38.870 --> 00:01:39.438
Interesting.

00:01:39.438 --> 00:01:40.730
Let's go back to the beginning.

00:01:40.730 --> 00:01:44.100
Were you always inclined
towards the sciences?

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:46.590
Absolutely.

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:51.900
I always had a sense of wanting
to know how things worked.

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:56.850
And even more than that, knowing
how the physical laws can

00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:59.440
be used to create new things.

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:02.080
And I think that's what
ultimately led me to AI,

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:02.580
you know.

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:05.880
How can we build solutions with
what we know about the world

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:07.900
and how things work?

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:11.440
So I've also had
this creative side,

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:14.440
and I think that's where
the two intersect is,

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:17.130
you know, wanting
to create something

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:21.370
not just to understand, but
to create from what we know.

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:22.770
Interesting.

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:24.870
So I understand from your
bio that you were also

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:25.860
once a firefighter.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:30.740
How did you make the segue
from firefighting into AI?

00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:33.830
Well, so firefighting
for me was a seasonal job

00:02:33.830 --> 00:02:40.110
where a lot of wildfires in the
summertime and the late spring.

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:44.450
So I did that during college
and grad school as a way

00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:47.960
to kind of save up
money for college,

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:52.160
and also to just have some
adventures outside of academia,

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:54.410
and to do, you know, good.

00:02:54.410 --> 00:02:58.355
To do something that would be
a positive thing on society.

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:04.100
So that was kind of
my side of adventure,

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:05.990
and I think that
adventurous side of me

00:03:05.990 --> 00:03:10.160
also is what led me to do
"Love is Blind" as well.

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:12.170
And what question or
challenge were you

00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:15.320
setting out to address when
you started your company?

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:18.740
Did you feel as though something
was lacking in the field?

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:20.570
Absolutely.

00:03:20.570 --> 00:03:22.490
One thing I've seen
in the field of AI

00:03:22.490 --> 00:03:27.270
is when companies
start to get bigger,

00:03:27.270 --> 00:03:31.470
they have a hard time moving
course or changing direction,

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:31.970
you know.

00:03:31.970 --> 00:03:34.790
They're kind of like
a train on a track

00:03:34.790 --> 00:03:37.250
where they've picked
up a lot of momentum

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:41.270
and they have a hard
time diverting course.

00:03:41.270 --> 00:03:46.910
I wanted to start a company that
was mobile and able to really

00:03:46.910 --> 00:03:50.030
tackle nuanced problems
that these bigger AI

00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:53.660
companies aren't able to do.

00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:55.910
And even more
specifically, there

00:03:55.910 --> 00:03:58.483
are certain problems
that I wanted to address,

00:03:58.483 --> 00:04:00.150
and that's why I
started my own company.

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:03.390
I wanted to be able to
work on these problems.

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:07.610
So my biggest interest is
in Parkinson's disease.

00:04:07.610 --> 00:04:10.460
I think there's a
lot of room there

00:04:10.460 --> 00:04:15.360
for implementing AI to
be able to do earlier

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:19.829
diagnosis on the disease,
potentially improve some

00:04:19.829 --> 00:04:23.750
of the treatments that
treat Parkinson's disease

00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:26.690
such as deep brain stimulation.

00:04:26.690 --> 00:04:31.070
I think there's room there to
optimize how DBS devices are

00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:33.060
used to treat Parkinson's.

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:37.060
So those are some
of my motivations

00:04:37.060 --> 00:04:39.550
for starting Alliance AI.

00:04:39.550 --> 00:04:40.600
That's great.

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:43.090
I want to talk a little bit
about digital dating now.

00:04:43.090 --> 00:04:45.580
Given that algorithms
are powering

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:48.220
much of our dating apps and
the focus there is largely

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:51.370
on physical appearance, did
your interest in the show

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:53.820
stem from a personal
belief that perhaps looks

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:56.020
are overemphasized?

00:04:56.020 --> 00:04:56.530
Yeah.

00:04:56.530 --> 00:04:59.980
So I was having that
experience myself

00:04:59.980 --> 00:05:05.320
where I was using the
apps and, admittedly, I

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:08.230
was doing it in kind
of a shallow way, which

00:05:08.230 --> 00:05:11.590
the apps kind of set you up
to do where you're seeing

00:05:11.590 --> 00:05:16.330
the pictures of the person,
or all the people on the app,

00:05:16.330 --> 00:05:20.080
and you're just
clicking yes or no.

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:20.800
I like them.

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:24.220
I don't like them, without
really thinking about them

00:05:24.220 --> 00:05:25.900
as a person, and
there's thousands

00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:27.040
of people on these apps.

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:31.200
So you're just kind of
going through yes and no.

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:34.550
And what I was
finding was, I was

00:05:34.550 --> 00:05:38.210
finding people who I thought
were physically attractive,

00:05:38.210 --> 00:05:40.460
but when I went
out on the dates,

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:43.340
there was no real
connection there,

00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:47.680
whether a shared interest
or an emotional connection.

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:50.160
So I was just kind
of running into a lot

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:54.150
of dating dead ends as it were.

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:58.500
So all the more reason I wanted
to try this experiment out

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:01.620
and see if, you know,
removing physical appearance

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:05.680
could be a positive boon.

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:08.010
But do you feel that this
structure possibly short

00:06:08.010 --> 00:06:11.640
changes some of the natural
and biological aspects

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:13.350
of attraction that
really only exist

00:06:13.350 --> 00:06:17.340
in a physical space like
pheromone transmission?

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:21.000
Well, I think it would
be unwise to deny

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:24.410
that physical
attraction is certainly

00:06:24.410 --> 00:06:27.720
a very, very important
part of a relationship.

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:29.080
We need it, frankly.

00:06:29.080 --> 00:06:31.200
We need to be
physically attracted.

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:35.940
Physical intimacy is a
necessity in a relationship.

00:06:39.070 --> 00:06:46.310
I think that we also need that
emotional connection as well.

00:06:46.310 --> 00:06:50.660
So you know, doing
this experiment,

00:06:50.660 --> 00:06:55.150
it was a case where
I was able to--

00:06:55.150 --> 00:06:57.940
well, let me put it like this.

00:06:57.940 --> 00:07:02.260
We were able to build
the emotional connection.

00:07:02.260 --> 00:07:05.710
For some, obviously, when
they got to see each other

00:07:05.710 --> 00:07:09.380
physically that physical
connection wasn't there.

00:07:09.380 --> 00:07:13.570
So it was a bit of a risk
to do it in this fashion,

00:07:13.570 --> 00:07:16.240
but it did allow us all
to focus on building

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:20.560
that emotional connection at
first kind of as a foundation.

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:22.030
But I think both are needed.

00:07:22.030 --> 00:07:24.640
That emotional connection,
the physical connection,

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:28.250
being aligned on
values, you know.

00:07:28.250 --> 00:07:30.490
How are you going to
raise children together?

00:07:30.490 --> 00:07:32.860
Are your spiritual beliefs
at least compatible

00:07:32.860 --> 00:07:37.480
enough so that you're not
butting heads constantly,

00:07:37.480 --> 00:07:39.380
especially if you're
going to raise children.

00:07:39.380 --> 00:07:42.340
And how are you going to
manage a household together?

00:07:42.340 --> 00:07:46.570
So there's so many different
things to consider when you're

00:07:46.570 --> 00:07:49.930
finding that lifetime partner.

00:07:49.930 --> 00:07:53.110
Did you entertain the
possibility of disappointment

00:07:53.110 --> 00:07:55.600
if you weren't physically
attracted to your fiancee

00:07:55.600 --> 00:07:57.470
when you first met her?

00:07:57.470 --> 00:07:57.970
Oh yeah.

00:07:57.970 --> 00:07:58.630
Of course.

00:07:58.630 --> 00:08:03.250
I mean there's-- in my
mind, there was a very real

00:08:03.250 --> 00:08:08.410
possibility that we wouldn't
be attracted to each other.

00:08:08.410 --> 00:08:11.110
Either she wasn't going
to be attracted to me,

00:08:11.110 --> 00:08:13.630
or I was going to
be attracted to her.

00:08:13.630 --> 00:08:16.930
But I thought that,
you know, since we

00:08:16.930 --> 00:08:21.970
have such a strong bond before
we've even seen each other,

00:08:21.970 --> 00:08:26.080
if there's less physical
attraction there than I would

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:29.860
want, I thought there
might be a possibility it

00:08:29.860 --> 00:08:31.090
could build over time.

00:08:31.090 --> 00:08:32.990
And I think that happens.

00:08:32.990 --> 00:08:37.330
I think that if you
love someone for reasons

00:08:37.330 --> 00:08:39.610
other than their
physical appearance,

00:08:39.610 --> 00:08:43.840
you can build even such
that you become more

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:46.000
physically attracted to them.

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:47.660
I definitely agree with that.

00:08:47.660 --> 00:08:50.250
How do you see AI shaping
the future of dating?

00:08:50.250 --> 00:08:53.290
We've kind of come a long
way since the first iteration

00:08:53.290 --> 00:08:54.000
of OkCupid.

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:54.500
Right.

00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:56.320
And there's so many
apps that are tailor

00:08:56.320 --> 00:08:57.610
made to different preferences.

00:08:57.610 --> 00:09:00.100
But with all your
experiences, how do you

00:09:00.100 --> 00:09:02.330
see the future of that?

00:09:02.330 --> 00:09:03.360
That's a great question.

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:08.140
So yeah, I started thinking
about the current state

00:09:08.140 --> 00:09:10.100
of dating apps.

00:09:10.100 --> 00:09:13.450
And what I've seen
and what I can assume

00:09:13.450 --> 00:09:17.200
is that they're using some
variation of collaborative

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:22.600
filtering to underlie
the algorithms there.

00:09:22.600 --> 00:09:25.250
Where, you know,
they're rating people

00:09:25.250 --> 00:09:28.900
and it's kind of a cold
start problem at first where

00:09:28.900 --> 00:09:31.750
the app doesn't know
the type of people

00:09:31.750 --> 00:09:34.630
that this person
is interested in.

00:09:34.630 --> 00:09:39.250
And then the apps
start to narrow down

00:09:39.250 --> 00:09:41.140
based on the history
of who they've said

00:09:41.140 --> 00:09:42.610
they liked and didn't like.

00:09:42.610 --> 00:09:45.700
But I think there's kind
of an inherent problem

00:09:45.700 --> 00:09:50.800
there where after using the
app for a period of time,

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:55.120
it starts to narrow the
scope of potential candidates

00:09:55.120 --> 00:09:59.230
so much that you're not
exploring these people

00:09:59.230 --> 00:10:04.930
outside of that recommended
group of people who actually

00:10:04.930 --> 00:10:09.010
may be a great complement
to you or a great match.

00:10:09.010 --> 00:10:13.320
So that's something
that I think could

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:14.970
be improved upon in the future.

00:10:14.970 --> 00:10:20.180
And also, perhaps an app
that was able to look at a

00:10:20.180 --> 00:10:25.260
means for incorporating
the emotional component

00:10:25.260 --> 00:10:29.030
the relationship and finding
someone who is a match there,

00:10:29.030 --> 00:10:32.900
or these other factors that
we mentioned such as managing

00:10:32.900 --> 00:10:37.790
a household together,
shared beliefs on family,

00:10:37.790 --> 00:10:40.550
and finances, and
these type of things.

00:10:40.550 --> 00:10:45.260
So a more comprehensive app
that was not simply looking

00:10:45.260 --> 00:10:48.320
at physical appearance, which I
still think is very important,

00:10:48.320 --> 00:10:55.010
but also emotional connection
and shared belief systems

00:10:55.010 --> 00:10:57.000
in these other domains as well.

00:10:57.000 --> 00:11:03.090
So there's just so much to
consider with a partner.

00:11:03.090 --> 00:11:07.530
I think that that's the future
of dating apps is something

00:11:07.530 --> 00:11:10.300
that's more comprehensive.

00:11:10.300 --> 00:11:12.690
And after this experience
on "Love is Blind," would

00:11:12.690 --> 00:11:16.140
you recommend this
format to other people.

00:11:16.140 --> 00:11:17.350
Oh yeah, absolutely.

00:11:17.350 --> 00:11:23.730
I would certainly recommend
the "Love is Blind" approach.

00:11:23.730 --> 00:11:25.410
With that being
said, I think it's

00:11:25.410 --> 00:11:29.940
all about you as an individual
and how you go into it.

00:11:29.940 --> 00:11:32.990
Just like dating in
any other format,

00:11:32.990 --> 00:11:36.860
there's a certain level of
openness and vulnerability

00:11:36.860 --> 00:11:40.610
that you'd need to be willing
to expose yourself to.

00:11:40.610 --> 00:11:45.640
So that's the main
takeaway I had was,

00:11:45.640 --> 00:11:47.390
if you want to make
this work, you kind of

00:11:47.390 --> 00:11:49.550
have to go into a prepared.

00:11:49.550 --> 00:11:52.880
You have to go into it
being willing to take

00:11:52.880 --> 00:11:55.710
the chance that
you may get hurt,

00:11:55.710 --> 00:12:00.440
and you have to be brave
enough to expose yourself

00:12:00.440 --> 00:12:04.660
emotionally and be
willing to give it a shot.

00:12:04.660 --> 00:12:05.756
Right.

00:12:05.756 --> 00:12:07.970
Now, pulling back a
little more generally,

00:12:07.970 --> 00:12:10.070
where do you see the
future of AI going?

00:12:10.070 --> 00:12:12.830
5, 10 years from now, whether
that's your personal goals

00:12:12.830 --> 00:12:16.010
or just more largely?

00:12:16.010 --> 00:12:17.000
Wow.

00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:20.980
So the future of
AI as I see it, I

00:12:20.980 --> 00:12:23.540
think it's going to
diverge from this vision

00:12:23.540 --> 00:12:25.520
that we see in science
fiction where there's

00:12:25.520 --> 00:12:31.080
some maleficent AI that
has a mind of its own

00:12:31.080 --> 00:12:32.390
and is taking over things.

00:12:32.390 --> 00:12:38.700
I think the real positive
trajectory for AI

00:12:38.700 --> 00:12:43.650
is to think about
how AI in human life

00:12:43.650 --> 00:12:45.390
can kind of integrate
in such a way

00:12:45.390 --> 00:12:50.180
that the AI is only augmenting
what we naturally do.

00:12:50.180 --> 00:12:54.160
It's giving us a better quality
of life on a day to day.

00:12:54.160 --> 00:12:58.050
You know, whether that's
improving the way that we

00:12:58.050 --> 00:13:02.490
schedule our day, whether
that's cutting down

00:13:02.490 --> 00:13:07.065
the amount of time we spend
doing sort of minute tasks.

00:13:09.930 --> 00:13:12.570
Basically, I see
the future of AI

00:13:12.570 --> 00:13:16.140
is giving us more
time to be creative

00:13:16.140 --> 00:13:20.550
by automating tasks,
allowing us to dig deeper

00:13:20.550 --> 00:13:25.580
by helping us to do
research, and helping us

00:13:25.580 --> 00:13:27.410
to be more creative in general.

00:13:27.410 --> 00:13:31.430
And hopefully to be
able to spend more time

00:13:31.430 --> 00:13:33.770
with other people and connect.

00:13:33.770 --> 00:13:35.240
That's a really good point.

00:13:35.240 --> 00:13:36.950
Well, thank you so
much for joining us.

00:13:36.950 --> 00:13:41.350
Do you have any parting words
or advice for our viewers?

00:13:41.350 --> 00:13:42.990
Wow.

00:13:42.990 --> 00:13:46.270
Parting advice, I
would say, you know,

00:13:46.270 --> 00:13:53.030
if you're worried about
dating, to first kind of figure

00:13:53.030 --> 00:13:57.540
out what you want in a
partner and really to dig deep

00:13:57.540 --> 00:14:01.560
and think, you know, what is
it about my potential partner

00:14:01.560 --> 00:14:04.950
that I'm going to really
need in the long term?

00:14:04.950 --> 00:14:12.570
And to be willing to be
vulnerable enough to let them

00:14:12.570 --> 00:14:18.150
know who you really are even
if they might reject that.

00:14:18.150 --> 00:14:22.440
So that would be my advice.

00:14:22.440 --> 00:14:26.700
And on AI front for
the general public,

00:14:26.700 --> 00:14:32.220
I would say to not
be afraid of the AI.

00:14:32.220 --> 00:14:33.690
The future of it
I don't think will

00:14:33.690 --> 00:14:37.540
resemble what we see in
sci-fi like the Skynet

00:14:37.540 --> 00:14:38.720
and this type of thing.

00:14:42.050 --> 00:14:43.760
We need to start
thinking about AI

00:14:43.760 --> 00:14:49.070
in terms of how we can use it
to augment our day to day life.

00:14:49.070 --> 00:14:49.670
That's great.

00:14:49.670 --> 00:14:50.970
Thank you so much.

00:14:50.970 --> 00:14:51.470
Yes.

